  <preface xml:id="meta_frontmatter-preface">
    <p>
      <em>Learning Statistics with JASP</em> covers the contents of an introductory statistics
      class, as typically taught to undergraduate psychology students. The book discusses
      how to get started in JASP as well as giving an introduction to data manipulation.
      From a statistical perspective, the book discusses descriptive statistics and graphing
      first, followed by chapters on probability theory, sampling and estimation, and null
      hypothesis testing. After introducing the theory, the book covers the analysis of
      contingency tables, correlation, <m>t</m>-tests, regression, ANOVA and factor analysis.
      Bayesian statistics is covered at the end of the book.
    </p>
    <p>
      Citation: Navarro, D.J., Foxcroft, D.R., and Faulkenberry, T.J. (2025).
      <em>Learning Statistics with JASP: A Tutorial for Psychology Students and Other Beginners</em>.
      (Version 2.0).
      <url href="https://learnstatswithjasp.com" visual="learnstatswithjasp.com">
        learnstatswithjasp.com</url>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Six years after its initial release, <q>Learning Statistics with JASP</q> continues
      to be widely used and cited, and I am grateful for the many positive messages I have
      received. However, the book has been in great need of a refresh, particularly with
      the many new updates to the JASP software that have happened in the past few years.
      In mid 2024, my graduate student Kylie Seifert began to help me refresh all the JASP
      screenshots to reflect the most current version. Thus, the book you're reading now
      reflects how JASP looks as of version 0.19.3<ellipsis/>much better than the old version
      from 2019! I am so grateful for her work this year.
    </p>
    <p>
      I am also grateful to readers who have contributed suggestions and identified typos,
      including Michael Katz, Oliver Schultheiss, and Rachel Stephens. As always, I invite
      readers everywhere to find ways to make this text better (including identifying the
      ever-present typos). This version builds on the wonderful previous work of Dani Navarro
      and David Foxcroft, without whose previous efforts a book of this quality would not be possible.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you find any issues with this PreTeXt version of the book, please raise them at
      <url href="https://github.com/PreTeXtBooks/JASPbook/issues"
           visual="github.com/PreTeXtBooks/JASPbook/issues">
        github.com/PreTeXtBooks/JASPbook/issues</url>.
    </p>
    <attribution>
      <line>Thomas J. Faulkenberry</line>
      <line>May 21, 2025</line>
    </attribution>
  </preface>

